# Intro
Reproduces the STARBENCH project's late-phase D-type expansion test
(Bisbas et al. 2015, MNRAS 453, 1324, arXiv:1507.05621), Section 4.1.2:
a single ionizing source at the center of a uniform pure-hydrogen medium,
density `rho_o = 5.21e-21 g/cm^3` (`n_H ~ 3115 cm^-3`), emitting
`Q_H = 1e49` photons/s. STARBENCH's own late-phase reference solution --
twelve independent radiation-hydrodynamics codes, mutually agreeing to
~1-2% -- is the "STARBENCH equation" (their Eqns 8, 11, 28, 29), not the
classical Spitzer (1978) D-type formula used by the sibling
`../StromgrenSphere/` example's `stromgren_analytic_check.py`. Spitzer
over-predicts the true expansion at late times (the paper's whole point);
STARBENCH's blend of the exact pressure-driven ODE (their "Raga-I", Eqn 8)
and the Hosokawa & Inutsuka (2006) shell-inertia ODE ("Raga-II", Eqn 11)
is the correct target to validate against.

Use `starbench_analytic_check.py` (not `stromgren_analytic_check.py`) to
compare this run's `r_HII(t)` against that reference solution.

## Temperature: Z=0, this code's own floors, not the papers' flat 1e4 K
STARBENCH assumes `T_i=1e4 K` (ionized) and `T_o=1e3 K` (neutral) by
construction of the test itself -- an idealized two-temperature medium no
participating code actually integrates via a real cooling network. This
code does not have that option: gas cools/heats via the normal Grackle
physics. `GEARChemistry:initial_metallicity=0` (literally pure hydrogen,
matching STARBENCH's own medium exactly) is used rather than the
`Z/Zsun~0.231` workaround this project's other radiation examples use to
hit the papers' flat 1e4 K target -- see "What actually happened" below
for why. At Z=0 this code's own metallicity-dependent
collisional-equilibrium fit (`radiation_get_T_collisional_K`) gives
`T_i~47500 K`, not 1e4 K. The STARBENCH comparison is evaluated at
whatever `T_i`/`T_o` this run actually used
(`starbench_analytic_check.py --T-ionized-K --T-neutral-K`, defaulting to
47500/1000), not hardcoded to the papers' own numbers -- this remains a
valid, precise check even though `T_i` doesn't match the papers'
convention.

## What actually happened (2026-07-19): the neutral gas was not
## staying anywhere near `T_o`
The first version of this example used `Z/Zsun=0.231` (to hit `T_i=1e4 K`
exactly) and left `SPH:minimal_temperature` at its usual value of 1 K,
assuming the mismatch between "neutral gas cools/heats freely" and
STARBENCH's "neutral gas held fixed at `T_o`" would be a minor, second-order
effect. It measured as the *dominant* one: a direct check of the completed
3 Myr run found untagged (neutral) gas sitting at **2-5 K** from the very
first snapshot (`t~0.05 Myr`) onward, not anywhere near `T_o=1000 K`.
Real metal-line cooling at `Z/Zsun=0.231` (absent from STARBENCH's
literal pure-H test) combined with no floor to stop it, and the gas just
kept cooling. A collapsed `T_o` collapses `c_o`
(`R_STAG = (c_i/c_o)^(4/3) R_St` -- STARBENCH Eqn 14), removing essentially
all the pressure confinement the late-phase stagnation radius depends on:
the simulated front kept growing well past the STARBENCH-predicted
radius instead of leveling off (`r_sim=5.96 pc` vs. `R_SB=2.25 pc`
predicted at `t=3 Myr`, a 165% miss).

Fix: `SPH:minimal_temperature=1000` (floors the neutral gas near `T_o`
without forcing a full isothermal EOS -- gas can still heat freely via
compression/shocks, it just can't cool below this) combined with `Z=0`
(removes the metal-line cooling that was driving the collapse in the
first place, and is the more literal reproduction of STARBENCH's
pure-H medium besides). This fix is in the committed `params.yml`
(`SPH:minimal_temperature=1000`, `initial_temperature=1000`,
`GEARChemistry:initial_metallicity=0`).

## Building to match STARBENCH's own `T_i` convention
`T_i` is set at compile time, not in `params.yml`. To force it to
STARBENCH's own flat `1e4 K` (see "Temperature" above for why this
differs from the plain build's natural, metallicity-dependent value):
```
CFLAGS+="-DIONIZATION_FEEDBACK_DEBUG_FIXED_IONIZED_TEMPERATURE_K=1e4" ./configure \
    --with-chemistry=GEAR_10 --with-feedback=GEAR --with-cooling=grackle_0 \
    --with-stars=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR \
    --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=$GRACKLE_ROOT
make -j$(nproc)
```
Then run `./run.sh` unmodified -- no other parameter changes needed,
same committed `params.yml`/`run.sh`. When checking the result, pass
the matching `--T-ionized-K` to `starbench_analytic_check.py` (see
"Usage" below) -- it must match whatever `T_i` the build actually used,
or the check compares against the wrong reference curve silently.

## Star mass
STARBENCH specifies `Q_H` directly, not a stellar mass. This code's
Q_H(mass) fit (`radiation_get_individual_star_ionizing_photon_emission_rate_fit`,
`src/feedback/GEAR/radiation.c`) gives `Q_H = 1e49 s^-1` at
`star_mass = 26.75 Msun` -- solved numerically once
(`starbench_analytic_check.py`'s own `ionizing_photon_rate()`, which is a
verbatim copy of the same fit used elsewhere in this project) and hardcoded
as `run.sh`'s default. Re-derive it the same way if the fit ever changes.
Unaffected by the Z=0 change (Q_H(mass) has no metallicity dependence).

## Resolution
`gas_mass=0.1 Msun` (default) gives ~770k particles at this box size and
density. At `Z/Zsun=0.231` (`T_i=1e4 K`) this was ~2.9 particles per
`R_St=0.319 pc`; at `Z=0` (`T_i~47500 K`), `R_St` grows to `0.507 pc`
(`alpha_B` drops ~4x at the higher temperature, `R_St ~ alpha_B^{-1/3}`),
giving ~4.6 particles per `R_St` instead -- slightly better resolved, not
worse. This is still coarser than the paper's own SPH tests (up to 8e6
particles, Section 4.2.1) -- a deliberate compromise for a repository
example that runs in a reasonable wall-clock time. Increase `gas_mass`
down for a tighter resolution test if needed.

## Box size
`boxsize=0.01` kpc (10 pc, 5 pc half-width) was sized against
`R_STAG~2.32 pc` at `T_i=1e4 K` -- i.e. for the debug build above,
with headroom to spare. At `Z=0`'s natural, un-forced `T_i~47500 K`
(the plain default build, no debug flag), `R_STAG` instead grows to
`~10.6 pc` (`c_i` alone grows ~2.2x, and `R_STAG ~ c_i^{4/3}`) --
comfortably beyond this box, so the plain build's comparison window is
limited to the time before `R_SB(t)` exceeds the 5 pc half-width
(`starbench_analytic_check.py` picks this automatically). Growing the
box to cover the natural-`T_i` case's full `R_STAG` would need a >60x
larger particle count at fixed resolution (mass in a uniform box scales
as `L^3`), not practical for a repository example.

## Gravity
`--external-gravity` is passed (matching every other example in this
family) but no `Potential:` block is configured in `params.yml`, so there
is no gravitational force in practice -- self-gravity is off entirely,
matching STARBENCH's own purely hydrodynamical test (Section 4, "The test
is purely hydrodynamical i.e. it includes no gravity or magnetic fields").

## Usage
Plain default build, this code's own natural `T_i`:
```
./run.sh
python3 starbench_analytic_check.py -s snap/snapshot_*.hdf5
```
To build and run with `T_i` forced to STARBENCH's own convention
instead, see "Building to match STARBENCH's own `T_i` convention"
above, then check with the matching `--T-ionized-K`:
```
./run.sh
python3 starbench_analytic_check.py -s snap/snapshot_*.hdf5 --T-ionized-K 1e4
```

Override any parameter via the matching env var, e.g.
`gas_mass=0.05 rebuild_time_myr=0.005 ./run.sh` -- see `run.sh` for the
full list.
